      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;div id="resg-s1-145106" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2244;         --source-height: 1320;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2244x1320+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F51%2F04%2Fb54976eb449884ae16d1b815aa30%2Fap26267781548857.jpg" class="img" alt="This satellite image from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (NOAA) shows Tropical Storm Nolo, left, south of Hawaii; Hurricane Odalys, middle, and Hurricane Polo, right, off the coast of Mexico, in the Pacific Ocean on Thursday, Sept. 24, 2026." fetchpriority="high" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 This satellite image from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration shows three storms in the Pacific Ocean on Thursday, Sept. 24, 2026. Tropical Storm Nolo, left, is south of Hawaii. Hurricane Polo, right, is off the coast of Mexico. Hurricane Odalys is in the middle.&lt;br&gt;                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          NOAA via AP/NOAA                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;It's been an extreme hurricane season in both the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans — but for vastly different reasons.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;The Pacific Ocean has seen 18 hurricanes so far and 41 &lt;a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/tcr/?season=2026&amp;basin=epac&amp;utm_source=gemini#:~:text=East%20Pacific%20Tropical%20Weather%20Summary%20(2026)%20Storm,Jul.%2035.%201003.%200.7.%205.%20Elida.%20TS." target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;named storms&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; overall, which are unusually high numbers for this point in the season.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-5980351-100" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-5980351-100" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/07/22/nx-s1-5902142/tropical-storm-bertha-hurricane-season" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Atlantic Ocean&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; is experiencing the exact opposite. &lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/hrd/hurdat/All_U.S._Hurricanes-aug2011.html" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;For the first time&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; in over 100 years, no full-blown hurricanes have formed in the Atlantic as of September 25. Only seven named storms have formed, all of which were less-intense tropical storms.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"It's a tale of two basins," says Gabriel Vecchi, a professor of geosciences at Princeton University. "The Pacific: incredibly active. The North Atlantic: incredibly inactive."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Scientists say this is likely due to a powerful El Niño climate pattern. This natural pattern, which occurs every few years, tends to suppress hurricanes in the Atlantic while fueling more storms in the Pacific.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;Hawaii is one place bearing the brunt of this shift.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/25/nx-s1-5981082/hurricane-nolo-hawaii" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hurricane Nolo&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; is expected to bring heavy rainfall and potential flooding to Hawaii this weekend, according to the &lt;a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/refresh/graphics_cp2+shtml/241449.shtml?key_messages#contents" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;National Hurricane Center&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;It's the third hurricane to threaten the islands this year, following Hurricane Lala in August and Hurricane Lowell in early September.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-145107" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 4818;         --source-height: 3212;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/900/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/1200/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/1600/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/1800/quality/85/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/jpeg" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/1100/quality/50/format/jpeg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4818x3212+0+0/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F72%2F30%2Febdf7753439588e5924cc0bec671%2Fap26268606705596.jpg" class="img" alt="Ryanna Fry takes a picture of clouds as Hurricane Nolo sits nearby in Kona, Hawaii, Friday, Sept. 25, 2026" loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Ryanna Fry takes a picture of clouds as Hurricane Nolo sits nearby in Kona, Hawaii, Friday, Sept. 25, 2026.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Gregory Bull/AP                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;b class="toggle-caption"&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/b&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The role of El Niño&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Why is it such an unusual year? Climate scientists point to this year's record-breaking El Niño.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;El Niño happens when water temperatures increase in the Pacific Ocean, near the equator. That warmer water provides fuel for hurricanes, driving bigger and more powerful storms. That means when a storm forms, it's more likely to reach hurricane strength.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-5980351-101" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-5980351-101" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;It also affects weather patterns around the globe. In the North Atlantic, El Niño tends to increase wind shear, making it more difficult for hurricanes to form by tearing storms apart.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;This year, El Niño is more intense than usual. Pacific water temperatures are already hitting record highs, even though the phenomenon won't reach its peak until later this year.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"We are headed toward what will almost certainly be the strongest El Niño on record," says Brian McNoldy, a senior research associate at the University of Miami. "It has exceeded expectations," in both the Pacific and the Atlantic, he says.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Warmer waters can also fuel more rapid intensification once storms form.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;On Tuesday, &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/23/nx-s1-5978589/hurricane-polo" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hurricane Polo&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, off Mexico's Pacific coast, strengthened from a tropical storm to a Category 5 hurricane in less than 24 hours.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Vecchi describes the intensification of Polo as "astounding."&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Such rapid intensification can make storms more dangerous, in part by limiting the time people have to prepare.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the role of climate change?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;Scientists are still trying to understand why some El Niño events are stronger than others. Some &lt;a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.ady2660" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;recent studies suggest&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/09/25/nx-s1-5953022/weather-super-rain-climate-extreme-godzilla-el-nino" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;climate change may be driving stronger &lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;El Niño events.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resnx-s1-5980351-102" class="bucketwrap internallink insettwocolumn inset2col"&gt;                     &lt;!-- END CLASS="BUCKET IMG" --&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;     &lt;!-- END ID="RESNX-S1-5980351-102" CLASS="BUCKETWRAP INTERNALLINK INSETTWOCOLUMN INSET2COL " --&gt;    &lt;p&gt;That science remains uncertain, Vecchi says, but the potential impacts of El Niño are well-understood.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/06/17/g-s1-128448/el-nino-begins-global-impacts" target="_blank"&gt;El Niño generally raises temperatures &lt;/a&gt;globally and can make some areas around the world wetter and others drier.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;In the immediate future, Paul Roundy, a professor of atmospheric science at the University at Albany, says he's watching for more storms to impact Hawaii along with droughts and fires in Central America.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;El Niño could also bring a wetter winter for parts of the Southwestern U.S., &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/08/15/nx-s1-5931512/lake-powell-and-lake-mead-water-levels-hit-record-lows" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;u&gt;where drought has persisted&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/a&gt;. But this could also increase the risk of floods.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;These impacts from El Niño come on top of warming driven by human-caused climate change, which is already increasing the likelihood of extreme weather around the world.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p&gt;"You can think of climate change as always acting in the background of basically everything we're experiencing in the ocean, in the atmosphere," McNoldy says. "So it tips the scales."&lt;/p&gt; &lt;/div&gt;</description>
      <media:content url="https://ondemand.npr.org/anon.npr-mp3/npr/atc/2026/09/20260925_atc_the_pacific_is_churning_with_hurricanes._the_atlantic_is_quiet._blame_el_ni_o.mp3?t=progseg&amp;amp;e=nx-s1-5950645&amp;amp;p=2&amp;amp;seg=10&amp;amp;d=189&amp;amp;size=3034009&amp;amp;sc=siteplayer&amp;amp;aw_0_1st.playerid=siteplayer" type="audio/mpeg"/>
